WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.860
Rise of Skywalker has fallen to the bottom
of the Star Wars pack.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.000
"I've got a bad feeling about this."

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:10.820
According to review aggregator Rotten Tomatoes,
the ninth and final film in the Skywalker

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:15.589
Saga has officially become the worst-reviewed
live-action Star Wars film of all time, edging

00:00:15.589 --> 00:00:18.720
out Star Wars: The Phantom Menace to take
over the bottom spot.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:25.400
Rise now sits at 52% on the old Tomatometer,
while Phantom Menace has a score of 53%.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:30.560
While the development isn't exactly surprising
given the extremely mixed reaction from audiences

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.130
and critics to Rise of Skywalker, it is rather
shocking, considering that the highly-anticipated

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:39.820
film closed out one the most beloved cinematic
sagas of all time not to mention the fact

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:45.430
that the preceding two films in the sequel
trilogy, 2015's The Force Awakens and 2017's

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:49.690
The Last Jedi, are both among the top four
most well-reviewed of all the Star Wars flicks

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:53.980
by professional critics, right alongside A
New Hope and The Empire Strikes Back.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.060
Obviously fans feel differently about The
Last Jedi, but we’re talking critics only

00:00:58.060 --> 00:00:58.560
here.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.340
Since initially posting a score in the high
50s, Rise of Skywalker's score has slowly

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:06.980
but surely sunk like a stone as reviews have
continued to come in.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.480
The movie, which saw the unexpected return
of Emperor Palpatine, wrapped up the character

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:16.030
arcs of Rey, Finn, and Poe, and served as
a final cinematic farewell to the late Carrie

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:21.160
Fisher, should have been a movie event for
the ages; instead, it largely failed to satisfy,

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:26.400
due in large part to the seeming insistence
of the filmmakers or, if certain online conspiracy

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:31.030
theories are to be believed, Disney on correcting
course from The Last Jedi and shoehorning

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.240
in egregious amounts of fan service.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.940
Rise can take solace in the fact that its
lowly rating pales in comparison to the animated

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:41.900
film The Clone Wars, with its miserable RT
score of 18%.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:46.679
When it comes to the question of what went
wrong with Rise, the only simple answer is,

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.160
"It wasn't just one thing."

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:52.960
While many fans weren't enthralled with some
of the, uh… bolder narrative choices made

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:58.260
by writer/director Rian Johnson in The Last
Jedi, Rise's seeming preoccupation with undoing

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.220
many of those choices largely wasn't seen
as a great move, either.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:06.080
Then, there's the return of Palpatine as the
Big Bad, with virtually no explanation given

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:11.472
as to a) how he survived a tumble down a mile-long
shaft on an exploding space station in Return

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.260
of the Jedi or b) where he's been all this
time.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.780
The about-face of Kylo Ren back to the side
of light was widely considered to have been

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:24.440
poorly and confusingly handled, and the near-exclusion
of Kelly Marie Tran's Rose Tico a major character

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.380
in The Last Jedi simply left a lot of critics
peeved.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:28.780
"May the Force be with you."

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:30.820
"Wow.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:32.060
You too."

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:36.520
It's also been pointed out, most succinctly
by the Washington Post's Gene Park, that the

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:41.390
movie's narrative resembles nothing so much
as the plot of a video game, with its over-reliance

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:45.640
on "fetch quests" which ultimately proved
to be of little to no actual importance.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:50.540
Truly, in contrast to the unified if divisive
vision of Star Wars creator George Lucas with

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:55.310
his much-reviled prequel trilogy, it took
a committee which included Lucasfilm president

00:02:55.310 --> 00:03:00.100
Kathleen Kennedy, director and co-writer J.J.
Abrams, co-writer Chris Terrio, and formerly

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:04.780
attached director Colin Trevorrow, who retained
a story credit to craft a conclusion to an

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:09.000
epic nine-film series as flat and uninspired
as Rise of Skywalker.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:13.820
Fortunately, though, the future of Star Wars
looks pretty bright if only because Lucasfilm

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:17.560
and Disney have more than one blueprint to
follow in order to get the franchise back

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:18.120
on track.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:23.060
First and foremost: the Disney+ series The
Mandalorian which has totally won over fans

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:24.060
and critics alike.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.360
In contrast to Rise of Skywalker, the series
has actually earned favorable comparisons

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:30.960
to well-crafted, well-plotted video game stories.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:35.210
Not to mention, its exploration of some of
the seedier portions of the galaxy far, far

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:39.710
away and its introduction of inspired new
characters such as the suddenly ubiquitous

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:44.640
Baby Yoda have reminded fans how compelling
Star Wars can be when storytellers like Mandalorian

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.320
creator Jon Favreau are given free rein to
put their own stamp on the property.

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:50.940
Second: the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:56.080
Marvel Studios is, of course, also owned by
Disney, and its decade-plus long existence

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:00.600
has been one long case study in how to craft
stories based on beloved properties, build

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:05.320
and sustain interest while indulging the muses
of an entire collective of unrelated creatives,

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:09.300
and wrap up extended narratives with event
films that feel like events.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:13.460
It's no coincidence that Marvel head honcho
Kevin Feige has been tapped to produce at

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:17.789
least one Star Wars film in the future, or
that Taika Waititi who directed the final

00:04:17.789 --> 00:04:21.709
episode of The Mandalorian's first season,
and helmed Thor: Ragnarok and the upcoming

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:26.330
Thor: Love and Thunder for Marvel is in line
to also direct a future Star Wars film, if

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:27.560
the rumors are to believed.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:31.680
Hopefully, the Mouse House will learn the
right lessons from Rise of Skywalker's lukewarm

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:36.629
reception from fans and critics and looking
to its most successful subsidiary for inspiration

00:04:36.629 --> 00:04:38.760
certainly doesn't seem like it could hurt.

